## Quick start

Build the application image:

```bash
make build
```

Start the complete stack:

```bash
make start
```

Open a Moleculer REPL connected to the running cluster:

```bash
make repl
```

Stop the stack:

```bash
make stop
```

## Make commands

| Command        | Description                                            |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| `make build`   | Build the application Docker image                     |
| `make start`   | Start the generated stack                              |
| `make stop`    | Stop the stack                                         |
| `make restart` | Restart the stack                                      |
| `make logs`    | Follow container logs                                  |
| `make repl`    | Open a REPL connected to the running Moleculer cluster |

## Moleculer REPL

The generated project includes a dedicated REPL container connected to the same Docker network and Moleculer transporter as the application services.

Start the stack first:

```bash
make start
```

Then open the REPL:

```bash
make repl
```

The REPL runs as a temporary Moleculer node.

It does not load application services locally. Instead, it joins the existing Moleculer cluster and discovers services through the Moleculer service registry.

This keeps the REPL connected to the same distributed topology used by the running application.

Useful commands include:

```text
mol $ nodes
mol $ services
mol $ actions
mol $ call <service>.<action>
```

For example:

```text
mol $ call articles.list
```

Leaving the REPL removes its temporary container automatically.

> `make repl` expects the application stack to already be running.

## Project structure

```text
.
├── docker/
│   └── services/          # Generated Docker Compose service definitions
├── src/
│   ├── config/            # Moleculer and application configuration
│   └── services/          # Moleculer application services
├── .env.example           # Generated environment template
├── .env.dev               # Default development environment
├── docker-compose.yaml    # Base Docker Compose configuration
├── Dockerfile
├── makefile
└── package.json
```

Each application service lives under `src/services`.

The Docker configuration is modular. Databases, transporters, plugins and application services provide their own Compose definitions under `docker/services`.

## Generating services

`moleculer-gen` can extend an existing generated project with additional microservices.

### Add a single service

Generate a service interactively:

```bash
npx moleculer-gen add-service
```

A single service can also be generated from a JSON configuration file:

```bash
npx moleculer-gen add-service path/to/service.json
```

### Add multiple services

Generate several services at once with `add-services`:

```bash
npx moleculer-gen add-services path/to/services.json
```

Example:

```json
{
  "services": [
    {
      "serviceName": "articles",
      "serviceDirectoryName": "articles",
      "isCrud": true,
      "exposeApi": true
    },
    {
      "serviceName": "comments",
      "serviceDirectoryName": "comments",
      "isCrud": true,
      "exposeApi": true
    }
  ]
}
```

Each entry follows the same service configuration rules as `add-service`.

When optional names are omitted, `moleculer-gen` generates them using the project naming conventions.

Each generated service is added as an independent Moleculer service with its own Docker Compose definition, preserving the generated project's one-container-per-service topology.

After adding services, rebuild and restart the stack:

```bash
make build
make restart
```

The new services join the existing Moleculer cluster through the configured transporter and can be discovered immediately from the REPL:

```bash
make repl
```

```text
mol $ nodes
mol $ services
mol $ actions
```

Services can still be created or modified manually when needed.

> Generated files remain regular project files. `moleculer-gen` provides the initial structure and conventions, but does not lock the project afterward.

## Environment configuration

Environment variables are used by both the application and Docker Compose.

The generated project contains:

```text
.env.example   # Template containing the generated configuration
.env.dev       # Default local development environment
```

The Makefile uses `.env.dev` by default:

```makefile
ENV_FILE ?= .env.dev
```

Any Make command can use another environment file by overriding `ENV_FILE`:

```bash
ENV_FILE=.env.production make start
```

The selected environment file is loaded by the Makefile and passed to Docker Compose.
